  "query_preview": "..A 500 mb low is currently over the Pac NW and will \npush eastward over the next couple of days. Northern Arizona is \nmostly in the gusty and drier south side of the low. Radar showed \nlight reflectivity showers pushing into far northwestern AZ this \nmorning, but so far rain gauges suggest much of the precip is not \nreaching the ground yet, but this should start to change this \nafternoon. As a cold front pushes into NW Arizona tonight and move \neastward to a Flagstaff-Kayenta line around Friday morning, winds \nwill be southwest with gusts 25-35 mph this afternoon. Winds remain \ngusty overnight into Friday, with the strongest winds along a narrow \nband just downwind from higher terrain. This includes parts of the \nKaibab Plateau, the far northeast portions of the Flagstaff area \n(Doney Park, Timberline), and parts of the White Mtns region. While \nthe GFS and ECMWF have backed off slightly on the extent of the \nwinds, gusts in these areas could still reach 40-50 mph, with most \nother areas peaking in the 30-40 mph range. Given the limited extent \nof the wind gusts within the models and recent trends in the GFS and \nECMWF, still could not support the issuance of a Wind Advisory. The \nmajority of precipitation is forecast to stay north of the state, \nshowers are likely tonight (60-80%) over the Kaibab Plateau, with \nlower chances farther south as moisture profiles are shallow closer \nto I-40. Snow levels are forecast to fall to the 6000-7000 ft range, \nwith 1-4\" forecast on the Kaibab Plateau. Elsewhere, accumulation \n(if any) is expected to be less than 1\". A trailing shortwave is \nforecast to move across the area Friday night into Saturday morning. \nMoisture profiles remain shallow, except across far eastern Arizona \nwhere there is a 20-40% chance for snow showers, especially over the \nWhite Mountains and Chuska Mountains in Apache county. Behind the\ncold front and shortwave trough, night time temperatures are \nexpected to drop, potentially bringing a hard freeze to most of \nnorthern Arizona Saturday night.\n\nDry but continued cool weather is expected Saturday afternoon \nthrough at least Tuesday as the latest model continues to support \nthe next trough passing to the north for Monday. There is more \nuncertainty in the weather pattern for Wednesday into Thanksgiving, \nwith the next trough possibly moving through. The ECMWF supports a \nmore northern track, similar to the current system or the next \ntrough on Monday, while the GFS shows a cut-off low developing as \nthe trough drops further south. For now, the NBM  has a 15-25% \nchance of rain and high elevation snow showers due to the higher \nuncertainty. The majority of the guidance shows light precipitation \nif any, but continue to check back for updates as we move into the \ntravel period for Thanksgiving.",
